Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Gothic period live-action, set in the 1840s.

FADE IN:

INT. INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

A single candle lights a bare plaster room. NARRATOR, a gaunt, pale man in his thirties with ink-black hair, hollow dark eyes, and a black frock coat, sits rigidly at a scarred table. A closed silver pocket watch lies before him.

(SOUND: the pocket watch ticks sharply in the silent room)

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

The candle flame trembles as NARRATOR leans toward an unseen listener.

(SOUND: wax spits softly; the ticking continues)

NARRATOR
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad?

NARRATOR raises one steady finger.

NARRATOR
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story. It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - MORNING

OLD MAN, frail and white-haired, sits propped in bed in a white nightshirt and burgundy robe. One pale blue eye is clouded by a milky film. NARRATOR enters with a breakfast tray.

(SOUND: porcelain rattles faintly on the tray; a clock ticks beyond the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

OLD MAN looks up. His filmed eye falls upon NARRATOR, whose warm smile tightens as his fingers dig into the tray.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! Yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

INT. INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR slowly closes his hand around the silver watch. His face remains composed, but a vein pulses at his temple.

(SOUND: the chair creaks beneath him; the watch ticks on)

NARRATOR
And so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

He smiles with grave satisfaction.

NARRATOR
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me—with what caution, with what foresight, with what dissimulation I went to work!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

OLD MAN, frail and white-haired in his white nightshirt, sleeps behind fastened shutters. The door opens a finger's breadth, revealing NARRATOR's dark eye and a shuttered lantern.

(SOUND: the latch whispers; distant floorboards settle)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently! Then I put in a dark lantern, all closed, that no light shone out, and thrust in my head.

NARRATOR inches through the opening. His movement is nearly imperceptible.

(SOUND: OLD MAN breathes evenly; a deathwatch beetle ticks within the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh, you would have laughed to see how cunningly I thrust it in! I moved it slowly—very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man's sleep.

NARRATOR eases open the lantern until a hair-thin ray touches OLD MAN's closed eyelid.

(SOUND: the lantern hinge gives a tiny metallic creak)

The ritual repeats through seven changes of candle and moon. Each midnight, NARRATOR withdraws without striking.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This I did for seven long nights—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work. It was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR sits back, savoring the precision of his account. His pale fingers tap beside the watch.

(SOUND: the ticking swells with unnatural clarity)

NARRATOR
Would a madman have been so wise as this? I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

His tapping stops.

NARRATOR
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. Never before had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The shuttered room is black as pitch. OLD MAN sleeps while NARRATOR steadily widens the door and slips his head through.

(SOUND: mattress ropes strain as OLD MAN breathes; the latch barely scrapes)

A chuckle escapes NARRATOR. OLD MAN starts upright.

(SOUND: bedsprings snap taut; the room falls silent)

NARRATOR reaches for the lantern fastening. His thumb slips.

(SOUND: a sharp click of tin)

OLD MAN
Who's there?

NARRATOR freezes in the doorway. OLD MAN clutches the sheets and stares blindly into the darkness.

(SOUND: wind moans in the chimney; deathwatch beetles pulse inside the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle. He was still sitting up in the bed listening—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

OLD MAN releases a low, stifled groan.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew it was the groan of mortal terror—the low sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well.

OLD MAN
It is nothing but the wind in the chimney. It is only a mouse crossing the floor.

NARRATOR's teeth gleam faintly in the doorway.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him and enveloped the victim.

NARRATOR opens the lantern by the smallest degree. A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, falls precisely upon the filmed eye.

(SOUND: the lantern hinge whispers; OLD MAN's breath catches)

The pale blue eye is open—wide, wide open. Nothing else of OLD MAN's face can be seen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick heartbeat begins beneath the silence)

INT. INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR covers the silver watch with a black handkerchief. Its muffled ticking persists beneath his palm.

(SOUND: the watch beats like a pulse enveloped in cotton)

NARRATOR
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense? There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

His hollow eyes flare with remembered triumph.

NARRATOR
I knew that sound well. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The lantern ray pins OLD MAN's open eye. NARRATOR holds perfectly still while the heartbeat throbs through the darkness.

(SOUND: the muffled heartbeat quickens)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

OLD MAN's fists knot in the sheets. NARRATOR scarcely breathes, keeping the ray motionless.

(SOUND: the heartbeat grows louder and faster; OLD MAN's breath shudders)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man's terror must have been extreme! It grew louder every moment. Yet, for some minutes longer, I refrained and stood still.

NARRATOR glances toward the adjoining wall. Panic overwhelms his triumph.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a yell, NARRATOR throws open the lantern and leaps into the room. OLD MAN shrieks once. NARRATOR drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: one piercing shriek; the bed crashes against the floor)

NARRATOR kneels upon the overturned bed and smiles. Beneath it, the heartbeat continues.

(SOUND: the heart beats on, heavy but muffled beneath the mattress)

The lantern flame burns lower. NARRATOR waits, pressing down with both hands.

(SOUND: the heartbeat falters, slows, then ceases)

NARRATOR removes the bed and examines OLD MAN's still body. He holds a hand over the silent chest for a long moment.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead. Yes, he was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The body lies beside the overturned bed. NARRATOR rolls up his black sleeves and places a tub and saw upon the floor.

(SOUND: the old house groans around him; the saw teeth rasp against bone)

Working behind the mattress, NARRATOR cuts off the head, arms, and legs. Each part drops into the waiting tub.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

NARRATOR pries up three floorboards, lowers the remains between the scantlings, and replaces each plank in its former place.

(SOUND: iron pries against wood; the final board knocks snugly into place)

He brushes dust into the seams. The floor is clean and unstained.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No human eye—not even his—could have detected anything wrong. There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

NARRATOR stands over the hidden body and buttons his black frock coat.

(SOUND: a distant bell tolls four)

A firm knocking rises from below. NARRATOR's head snaps toward the door.

(SOUND: three measured knocks at the street door)

NARRATOR smiles.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I went down to open it with a light heart—for what had I now to fear?

INT. ENTRANCE HALL - PRE-DAWN

NARRATOR descends with the lantern and opens the street door. OFFICER REYNOLDS, lean and middle-aged with gray side-whiskers and a dark blue greatcoat, stands with OFFICER HALE, broad and brown-bearded, and OFFICER BRIGGS, young and fair-haired.

(SOUND: the final knock echoes through the hall; leather boots shift on the stone step)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play has been aroused. We have been deputed to search the premises.

NARRATOR smiles and throws the door wide.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Gentlemen, welcome. Search—search well.

The officers enter. Carrying the lantern, NARRATOR leads them through the hall and up the staircase.

(SOUND: four sets of footsteps climb the creaking stairs)

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The bed has been restored. OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HALE, and OFFICER BRIGGS search the chamber while NARRATOR displays OLD MAN's treasures, secure and undisturbed.

(SOUND: drawers open and close; boots pass over the concealed floorboards)

Satisfied, the officers sit in chairs brought by NARRATOR. He places his own chair directly over the hidden remains.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape across the boards; the officers begin an easy, muted conversation)

NARRATOR answers cheerily. The officers chat of familiar things, entirely at ease.

A faint ringing enters NARRATOR's hearing. His smile stiffens and his face loses color.

(SOUND: a thin metallic ringing rises beneath the conversation)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears. It continued and became more distinct—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

NARRATOR talks more freely, then faster. His words blur beneath a low, quick beat.

(SOUND: a muffled heartbeat emerges beneath the ringing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR gasps. The three officers remain relaxed and apparently hear nothing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not.

NARRATOR springs up, arguing about trifles in a high voice. His hands slash through the air.

(SOUND: the heartbeat steadily increases; the officers' quiet conversation continues)

NARRATOR paces the room with heavy strides, always crossing the boards above the body.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone?

He seizes his chair, swings it aside, and grates its legs violently across the floor.

(SOUND: wood shrieks against wood, but the heartbeat rises over it)

The officers continue to smile. Sweat streams down NARRATOR's pallid face.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

NARRATOR claws at his hair. The three officers watch him as the pounding becomes unbearable.

(SOUND: the heartbeat swells to a deafening, frantic tattoo)

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The officers' smiles vanish. OFFICER REYNOLDS looks down at the boards beneath NARRATOR's trembling hand.

(SOUND: the heartbeat cuts to silence)

INT. INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR sits motionless at the scarred table, his gaunt face drained of triumph. The black handkerchief still covers the silver watch.

(SOUND: one muffled beat beneath the cloth)

NARRATOR slowly lifts the handkerchief. The watch has stopped. He clamps a hand over his own heart as the candle gutters out.

(SOUND: a final heartbeat, then darkness)

FADE OUT.

> THE END <
